(project pp-cron
 (* agent note (|2026-11-10, design session with the operator; chapter
    authored in ~/dev/wyo.tech/sundial (an otherwise empty, non-git
    workspace), NOT yet in the tuna repo. Nothing here is built; every
    stanza is planned and the status field says so. Provenance: the
    session settled four things in this order - an agent-first cron in
    OCaml; tuna as the only central; cron runs as their own data type on
    tuna (not claims, not the interpreter runs table); and the three
    complexity candidates dialed back (no dispatch, no Postgres from the
    box, no DBOS). The split-book question stays open: this file is
    written standalone so it can move to a pp-cron repo unchanged, with
    section ingest as the half meant to be lifted into tuna's borg/ next
    to trace.borg and journal.borg if the operator wants it there.|) *)
 (doc "pp-cron: an agent-first cron for the fleet. Schedule, execute, and record every firing on the box; ship the record to one central; treat the absence of a record as data. The agent lives at the triage end, never in the execution path.")
 (status planned)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (no-inline)

 (section law-local-execution
  (doc "No firing depends on anything off-box, ever")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        The law, stated so it can be tested: with DNS dead, the network
        unplugged, and tuna unreachable, every job still fires and every
        run still lands in the local store. Shipping is a separate loop
        in a separate failure domain whose only job is to drain a table.

        Motivating incident (gregor, 2026-10-30): rc.d autolith_webui sat
        in state 'failed' and nothing served the console port for ~38h.
        No alert fired, because "no data arrived" was not an alertable
        event anywhere in the stack. Every probe in the stack was
        push-shaped and the pushes had stopped. See section heartbeat for
        the mechanism that turns that silence into a verdict.

        Consequence for the design: the scheduler may not block on a
        socket, may not read a token over the network at fire time, and
        may not consult central for any decision. If a future feature
        cannot be implemented without breaking this, it belongs at
        central instead.
       |))))

 (section run-record
  (doc "One record definition; the local schema, the wire JSON, and the tuna table are all derived from it")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        The record is the product. Everything else in this book exists to
        produce it faithfully or to move it. Defined once in lib/spec.ml:

          type job = {
            name : string ;
            schedule : Schedule.t ;        (* cron expr + explicit tz *)
            cmd : string list ;
            env_file : string option ;     (* local, 0600 *)
            timeout : float ;              (* seconds *)
            overlap : [ `Skip | `Queue | `Kill of float ] ;
            on_miss : [ `Skip | `Once | `All ] ;
            retry : (int * float) option ; (* attempts, delay seconds *)
            heartbeat : float ;            (* expected max gap, seconds *)
            tags : string list ;
          }

          type run = {
            box : string ;                 (* stable id, not hostname *)
            run_id : string ;              (* ULID, minted on the box *)
            job : string ;
            occurrence : float ;           (* intended fire, unix epoch *)
            started : float ;
            finished : float option ;
            exit : int option ;
            signal : int option ;
            verdict : [ `Ok | `Failed of string | `Timeout | `Missing ] ;
            attempt : int ;
            log_tail : string ;            (* bounded, 8 KiB *)
          }

        Field laws, each of which has a reason:

        . box is a stable identifier assigned at install, never a
          hostname. Hostnames change, get reused, and collide across
          providers; a record keyed on hostname silently merges two boxes.
        . run_id is a ULID minted on the box before the child starts, so
          the id exists even if the daemon dies mid-run. Time-sortable,
          which makes the outbox and the central table index-friendly.
        . occurrence is the INTENDED wall-clock fire time, not the actual
          start. A run that started 40s late still names the occurrence it
          served, so gap analysis counts occurrences and never has to
          guess. Two rows may share job+occurrence across attempts; they
          differ in run_id and attempt.
        . attempt counts within an occurrence, 1-based. Same occurrence
          keeps the same run_id across retries and differs only in
          attempt, so a job's own idempotency key has one obvious value.
        . `Missing is a central-side constructor. A box never sends it. If
          a box could send it, the absence of data would be
          indistinguishable from data saying there was no work - which is
          the failure this book exists to kill.

        The executor exports the same facts to the child as environment
        (section executor), so the record and the job see one truth.
       |))))

 (section schedule-files
  (doc "One file per job on local disk; s-expressions; validated before the daemon will use them")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        /etc/pp-cron/jobs/<name>.sexp, one job per file, read at start
        and on SIGHUP. No database, no network, no history. S-expressions
        because the operator reads these by hand at 3am and because the
        grammar costs 80 lines in lib/spec.ml:

          (job "acme-renew"
            (schedule "17 3 * * *")
            (tz "UTC")
            (cmd "/usr/local/bin/acme-client" "-n" "pricklypear.club")
            (timeout 900)
            (overlap skip)        ; skip | queue | kill
            (on-miss once)        ; skip | once | all
            (retry 2 300)         ; attempts, delay seconds
            (heartbeat 93600)     ; expected max gap: 26h for a daily job
            (tags ("tls" "club")))

        Validation law: unknown keys and unknown values are REFUSED, not
        ignored. A typo'd (overlap skpi) that silently defaults to the
        wrong policy is how a poison job loops forever. check refuses the
        whole file and names the line.

        Heartbeat coherence: a job's declared heartbeat must be at least
        its schedule cadence, otherwise central synthesizes false missing
        verdicts for a healthy job. check computes the cadence from the
        cron expression and refuses a heartbeat smaller than it.

        `pp-cron check` parses and validates the whole directory and exits
        nonzero on any error. It runs before rcctl restart and in CI, and
        the daemon applies the same validation on reload: a bad file at
        reload keeps the PREVIOUS job set running and logs the refusal.
        Fail closed on config means keep executing the last known-good
        set, never an empty set.

        Nothing secret goes in these files. They are world-readable
        configuration; secrets live in env files (below).
       |))))

  (subsection policies
   (doc "overlap, on-miss, retry: the three things cron does not have and this tool exists for")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         overlap governs a firing whose predecessor is still running:
         skip (default; the missed firing is RECORDED as skipped, not
         silently dropped), queue (serialize, bounded queue of one), kill
         (terminate the old process group after N seconds, record the old
         run as killed-by-policy). The default is skip because the
         pathology worth preventing is unbounded pile-up: one PP card
         burned 399 of 617 runs in a poison loop, and an overlap queue
         with no bound is the same bug with better manners.

         on-miss governs firings whose time elapsed while the box was off
         or the daemon was down: skip (default for anything periodic -
         a scraper does not need 12 catch-up runs), once (one catch-up
         firing, for renewals and anything that must catch up to now),
         all (never use it; it exists because refusing to implement a
         policy is how tools grow folklore).

         retry governs a failed attempt within the same occurrence:
         attempts and delay, recorded on the run row. Retry only covers
         the process failing; a non-zero exit is a failure unless the job
         declares ok-codes.

         Auto-disable is REFUSED as a policy. pp-cron never stops running
         a job because it keeps failing: a disabled job is an invisible
         outage, indistinguishable from a removed job, and it removes the
         evidence that would explain it. Persistent failure is escalated
         with its record (section triage); disabling is a human decision
         that lands as a file change with a diff.
        |))))

  (subsection env-and-secrets
   (doc "Per-job env from a local 0600 file; never from central")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         (env-from "/etc/pp-cron/env/acme-renew.env") is parsed as
         KEY=VALUE, no shell expansion, no quoting subtleties, and the
         file must be mode 0600 owned by the daemon user or the job is
         refused with a named error. A job runner concentrates every
         credential on the box; the one thing it must not do is widen
         their reach to a network service. Central holds no secrets for
         the box, in either direction.

         Environment is inherited minimal: PATH, HOME, TZ from the job's
         declared tz, plus the RUN_ID contract. no TERM, no LOGNAME, no
         shell rc reading.
        |))))

 (section scheduler
  (doc "A min-heap on a monotonic clock; the loop touches nothing but local time and SQLite")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        State: a min-heap of next fire times keyed on CLOCK_MONOTONIC -
        wall clock is used only to COMPUTE an occurrence, never to wait on.
        A wait computed against wall clock is a wait that storms forward
        when NTP steps the clock, and cron's own history with that bug is
        long. On a wall-clock step the scheduler recomputes the next
        occurrences from the persisted last-fire state instead of trusting
        its sleep deadline.

        Timezones: each job declares its tz and the occurrence is computed
        in that timezone with an explicit DST rule - a 03:17 daily job in a
        zone that skips 02:00-03:00 is a per-job decision, not an accident
        of the runtime's localtime. Default tz is UTC and the file must say
        so explicitly if it wants local time.

        The loop order is fixed: pop due, check overlap, insert running row
        (one transaction), dispatch, compute next fire, repeat. It never
        waits on a child, never on a socket, never on a lock it does not
        own.

        Single instance is enforced by flock on the pidfile (section
        deployment); a second start exits 0 with a message rather than
        fighting for the heap.
       |))))

 (section executor
  (doc "fork/exec in a fresh process group, deadline, group kill, log to a spool file")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Spawn: fork, setpgid in the child, exec the argv directly (no
        shell unless the job declares one). A fresh process group is what
        makes the deadline enforce anything: killing a child that spawned
        a grandchild otherwise leaves the grandchild running and the job
        appears to succeed while its work continues.

        Deadline: SIGTERM to the group, grace period (default 10s),
        SIGKILL to the group, reap. Verdict timeout, and the row records
        which signal ended what.

        Output: the child's stdout+stderr go to a spool file under
        /var/db/pp-cron/spool/, never a pipe. A pipe deadlocks the child
        the moment a chatty job fills the 64 KiB buffer and nobody is
        reading; a spool file costs one disk write and bounds the problem
        to disk. At reap, the tail (default 8 KiB) plus total byte count
        go into the record and the spool file is removed. The byte count
        matters: "8 KiB retained of 1.2 GiB" tells the reader the tail is
        not the whole story.

        Environment contract, the only durability hook pp-cron offers:

          RUN_ID            stable across retries of one occurrence
          PP_CRON_JOB       job name
          PP_CRON_OCCURRENCE  intended fire, unix epoch
          PP_CRON_ATTEMPT   1-based
          PP_CRON_BOX       stable box id

        Same RUN_ID across retries is the point: a job that needs
        idempotency (an upload, a payment, a card transition) keys on it
        and becomes safe to retry without the scheduler knowing anything
        about its internals. Anything stronger requires owning the job's
        control flow, which is out of scope by section non-goals.
       |))))

 (section store
  (doc "/var/db/pp-cron/runs.db: SQLite is the source of truth, and a run row commits with its outbox row")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        SQLite, WAL mode, synchronous=NORMAL, one writer (the daemon). The
        local schema is PRIVATE to the daemon and versioned with user_version
        - it is not the wire format. The wire format is the record in
        section run-record, serialized explicitly, so a local migration
        never becomes an ingest break.

          runs (run_id text PRIMARY KEY, box, job, occurrence real,
                started real, finished real NULL, exit_code int NULL,
                signal int NULL, verdict text, failure_class text NULL,
                attempt int, log_tail text, log_bytes int NULL,
                running int NOT NULL DEFAULT 0)
          outbox (run_id text PRIMARY KEY, payload text NOT NULL,
                  created_at real, attempts int DEFAULT 0)
          heartbeats (id integer PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT, ts real,
                      jobs_loaded int, pending_outbox int, in_flight int)
          dead_letter (run_id text PRIMARY KEY, payload text, reason text,
                       at real)
          meta (k text PRIMARY KEY, v text)

        Transaction law: a terminal run row and its outbox row commit in
        ONE transaction. A run row without a matching outbox row is
        unshippable and invisible, which is precisely the class of failure
        this design exists to eliminate. This is the one idea worth taking
        from DBOS (its transact pattern: state change and its queued
        effect in one commit).

        Shipping law: unsent outbox rows are NEVER pruned. Retention prunes
        terminal runs older than N days (default 30) subject to a size
        cap, and heartbeats older than M days (default 90, they are tiny).
        A row whose payload could not be delivered lives in dead_letter
        with a reason and is surfaced in `pp-cron status`; it is dropped
        only by an explicit human command.

        A `running` row exists while a child is alive so that overlap
        checks and crash forensics both have local truth. Running rows are
        not shipped (they would churn); a daemon that dies mid-run leaves
        the row, and the next start marks it interrupted (verdict failed,
        failure_class daemon-restart) rather than pretending it finished.
       |))))

 (section outbox
  (doc "A dumb batch shipper in its own failure domain; blind retries are safe by construction")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Loop: take up to 200 pending rows oldest-first, POST, delete on
        2xx, exponential backoff with jitter on 5xx and transport errors
        (1s doubling to a 5m ceiling), and on a 4xx that repeats three
        times move the payload to dead_letter and log it. Never drop
        silently, never block a firing, never grow the queue by retrying
        in a tight loop.

        Retries are blind because the receiver is idempotent: tuna's
        UNIQUE (box, run_id) plus upsert makes a duplicate post a no-op
        and a later post of the same run a supersede. The shipper does not
        track delivery state beyond "still in outbox", which is what keeps
        it dumb enough to be reliable.

        A Sink module with one HTTP implementation. A second sink
        (section non-goals) changes nothing above it: same table, same
        loop, same blind retry.

        Backpressure is bounded, not unbounded: if pending exceeds a
        threshold the daemon logs loudly and `status` says so. A box whose
        network has been down for a month should be visibly queued, not
        silently absorbing.
       |))))

 (section heartbeat
  (doc "The dead-man switch: silence becomes a verdict")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Two independent signals, because they fail independently.

        Box liveness: every 60s the daemon posts
        {box, ts, uptime_s, jobs_loaded, pending_outbox, in_flight}
        to POST /api/cron/heartbeat, independent of any job. Central sweeps
        for boxes whose last heartbeat is older than 3x their declared
        interval and opens an incident. This catches a dead daemon - the
        gregor 38h case - where per-job analysis cannot, because a dead
        daemon produces no job rows at all and "no rows" is
        indistinguishable from "job removed".

        Schedule snapshot: on start and on every successful reload, the
        daemon posts its parsed job set (name, schedule, tz, heartbeat
        policy, timeout) to POST /api/cron/jobs, which replaces that box's
        snapshot. Central needs the cadence to reason about what SHOULD
        have fired; deriving it from observed rows is circular and would
        make a job that stopped firing look like a job that never existed.

        Absence synthesis: central compares each job's expected occurrence
        series against observed cron_runs and inserts rows with
        verdict='missing' for occurrences that never arrived, once they are
        older than the job's declared heartbeat. Those rows are
        synthesized only when the box was ALIVE (heartbeat present) for
        that window; a box whose daemon was down gets one incident about
        the daemon, not four hundred missing-run rows about its jobs. The
        distinction between "the box was dead" and "this job was dead" is
        the whole value of having both signals.
       |))))

 (section ingest
  (doc "tuna owns the central record: its own table, its own endpoints, write-only")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        OPERATOR DECISION (2026-11-10): tuna is the only central. It is
        already OCaml/dune, already behind relayd with TLS on
        pricklypear.club, already has identities and bearer tokens, already
        has Postgres. One ingest endpoint and one table beats standing up a
        new service, and a second central means two notions of "did this
        fire" to reconcile - the exact bug class this project exists to
        kill. PP gets a read-only view of this data later (subsection
        lens); it does not get a second ingest path.
       |))))

  (subsection not-claims
   (doc "Cron runs are not claims and not tree rows")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         A claim in tuna is identified by its PATH and is a publication act:
         a citable, re-derivable assertion, enumerated by /claims. A cron
         run is telemetry - high cardinality, expiring, cited by run_id and
         not by path. Two lifecycles, two tables. When a claim needs to
         refer to a run it cites the run_id in its body.

         The mechanical reason is stronger than the conceptual one. The
         deployed floor (g1) gates tree ops on the caller's path_prefix and
         ignores prim, so read-without-write is not expressible there, and
         raw string prefixes collide ('u.agora' covers u.agora-rogue.*). A
         per-minute write stream behind that floor forces every box to hold
         read+write on its whole prefix and the laptop identity to hold a
         prefix covering every box. Revisit only if the auth rework lands
         read-only grants; the volume and lifecycle arguments survive it.
        |))))

  (subsection not-runs
   (doc "Not the interpreter runs table either")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         tuna's runs (0001) are interpreter runs: program_hash NOT NULL
         REFERENCES programs(hash), fuel, size_cap, step_count,
         result_hash, journal chain, verify_status, run_traces. A process
         run has none of that and would contribute eight nulls plus a
         filter on every existing query and page. Sharing the table would
         also mean sharing the id space with the calculus and its replay
         verifier. Separate table, separate module, separate endpoints -
         the same call trace.borg made for its own summary rows.
        |))))

  (subsection schema
   (doc "migrations/0018-cron-runs.sql: idempotent DDL, self-registering, as 0001-0017 do it")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS cron_runs (
           id uuid PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
           box text NOT NULL,                  -- stable box id
           run_id text NOT NULL,               -- ULID from the box
           job text NOT NULL,
           occurrence timestamptz NOT NULL,    -- intended fire
           started timestamptz NOT NULL,
           finished timestamptz NULL,
           exit_code int NULL,
           signal int NULL,
           verdict text NOT NULL,              -- ok|failed|timeout|missing
           failure_class text NULL,
           attempt int NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,
           log_tail text NULL,
           log_bytes bigint NULL,
           caller uuid NULL REFERENCES identities(id),
           received_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
           UNIQUE (box, run_id));

         CREATE INDEX cron_runs_box_idx ON cron_runs (box, occurrence DESC);
         CREATE INDEX cron_runs_job_idx ON cron_runs (job, occurrence DESC);

         CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS cron_heartbeats (
           box text NOT NULL,
           ts timestamptz NOT NULL,
           uptime_s bigint NULL,
           jobs_loaded int NULL,
           pending_outbox int NULL,
           in_flight int NULL,
           PRIMARY KEY (box, ts));

         CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS cron_jobs (
           box text NOT NULL,
           job text NOT NULL,
           schedule text NOT NULL,
           tz text NOT NULL,
           heartbeat_s int NOT NULL,
           timeout_s int NOT NULL,
           tags text[] NOT NULL DEFAULT '{}',
           seen_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
           PRIMARY KEY (box, job));

         CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS cron_rollups (
           box text NOT NULL, job text NOT NULL, day date NOT NULL,
           fired int NOT NULL, ok int NOT NULL, failed int NOT NULL,
           missing int NOT NULL,
           PRIMARY KEY (box, job, day));

         UNIQUE (box, run_id) is the entire idempotency story: the shipper
         retries blindly and the ingest upserts ON CONFLICT (box, run_id)
         DO UPDATE. received_at is server time and the only column central
         invents; everything else is the box's word.

         Retention: a daily rollup job folds raw rows older than 90 days
         into cron_rollups and deletes them, the move run_traces labels as
         GC-able bulk data. "Did this fire every day for a year" survives;
         the log tail does not, which is the right trade for the tail.
        |))))

  (subsection surface
   (doc "Four write endpoints and a read surface; no dispatch")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         POST /api/cron/runs        batch ingest, idempotent upsert
         POST /api/cron/jobs        replace one box's schedule snapshot
         POST /api/cron/heartbeat   liveness
         GET  /api/cron/runs        ?box= &job= &since= &verdict= , paged
         GET  /api/cron/jobs        expected cadences per box
         GET  /api/cron/summary     per box/job: last run, last ok, gap,
                                    pending outbox, open incidents

         Write-only from the box's perspective. There is NO dispatch
         endpoint and no command surface in v1: the moment central can
         name a command, a central compromise is remote code execution on
         every installed box with whatever privilege the daemon has. Remote
         dispatch needs signed job bundles and its own threat model
         (section non-goals), and it is not needed for the motivating
         failures.
        |))))

  (subsection identity
   (doc "One tuna identity per box; caller comes from the token, and box must match it")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Each box is minted an identity the way marin was minted: a name, a
         bearer token, no garden. Ingest authenticates on the bearer and
         sets caller from it; box in the payload must equal the caller's
         identity name or the batch is refused whole (400, no partial row
         write). So gregor's token cannot write wyo rows, and a compromised
         box can forge rows about itself and nothing else.

         Tokens are per-box, revocable by setting identities.token_hash to
         a new value and rcctl restart, exactly as the root rotation
         procedure already does. The box reads its token from a 0640 file
         at startup and fails closed if it is missing. Tokens never appear
         in logs, in the schedule files, or in the record.
        |))))

  (subsection lens
   (doc "PP sees cron_runs read-only through the lens; it is not a sync participant")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Later, not v1. The board wants run rows next to cards, so a
         read-only projection of cron_runs into PP prims (space series,
         own type) served through the lens is the shape. Direction matters:
         space rows WRITTEN INTO the lens substrate are force-pulled and
         superseded (the gregor-memory lesson), so the lens is a reader
         here and tuna is the writer. Nothing about that projection can
         make a job fire or a row ship.
        |))))

 (section triage
  (doc "Agent-first means the agent reads the record, never the scheduler")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        The boundary, stated as a hard line: no agent, model, or remote
        service is consulted before or during a firing. Nothing on the
        firing path is probabilistic, and no job can be delayed, skipped,
        or started because a remote thing was thinking.

        Where the agent earns its keep is after the fact. A verdict of
        failed, timeout, or missing with no acknowledged triage opens a PP
        card or an agent run with the run row attached (log tail, byte
        count, attempt history, sibling runs of the same job). The agent
        reads the log, decides fix / escalate / annotate, and writes that
        decision somewhere durable. It cannot disable a job, edit a
        schedule, or retry on its own in v1; those need signed bundles.

        Deduplication matters more than intelligence here: one openness
        per (box, job, failure_class) window, not one per failing run. The
        399-run poison loop produced 399 records and one bug; triage must
        open one card for it.
       |))))

 (section deployment
  (doc "rc.d, unprivileged by default, named-command privilege escalation")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        OpenBSD first (gregor, pricklypear.club, wyo.town), Linux where
        needed. A single static binary from dune, no runtime deps:
        pp-cron.exe.

        . user _ppcron, /sbin/nologin, no home
        . /etc/pp-cron/jobs/ (0755), /etc/pp-cron/env/ (0700), token 0640
        . /var/db/pp-cron/ owned by _ppcron (runs.db, spool/)
        . rc.d/ppcron with daemon_flags, flock on the pidfile
        . newsyslog entry: keep 7 rotations, the daemon reopens on SIGUSR1

        Privilege: the daemon runs unprivileged and stays unprivileged. A
        job that needs root declares it and the schedule is checked at load
        against /etc/doas.conf, where the named command is permitted
        explicitly. Never a general doas rule for the daemon user: the
        privileged commands are a reviewed list on disk, not a property of
        the runner. This is the same class of decision as the PP
        supervisor's unprivileged-identity setting, made at install time
        instead of discovered at 3am.

        Upgrades: install a new binary, `pp-cron check`, rcctl restart. The
        store migrates forward on start under user_version and refuses to
        start on a newer schema than it knows.
       |))))

 (section non-goals
  (doc "Refused in v1, with the seam where each could return")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Per-box web server. A loopback unix socket serving status is the
        whole surface, and the `status` subcommand reads it. A fleet of
        small HTTP servers is a fleet of things to harden, patch, and
        monitor, in service of a dashboard nobody asked for.

        Remote dispatch. Seam: a signed job bundle verified by the daemon,
        with its own threat model. Not v1, not implied by ingest.

        Postgres from the box. Central store on Postgres: yes, tuna already
        has it. The box talking Postgres directly: no - a credential that
        can write the record table on every box, 5432 reachability as a
        design constraint on central's topology, and schema coupling where
        a daemon compromise becomes a central write primitive. Seam: the
        Sink module, with insert-only per-box roles if it ever earns its
        place. Revisit after a thousand shipped runs.

        DBOS or any embedded durable-execution engine. DBOS is a library
        embedded in the program being made durable (Python, TypeScript,
        Go, Java, Rust), and its scheduled-jobs feature competes with this
        tool rather than plugging into it. Baking it in collapses to
        reimplementing deterministic replay and step checkpointing in OCaml,
        or requiring every job to be a DBOS app with a Postgres on every
        box. Durability is a property of the job's code, not of the
        scheduler: pp-cron offers RUN_ID and attempt, and a job that needs
        step-level resumption should be written in a language that has it
        and treated as a dumb process that exits. That composition is the
        point.

        Workflow DAGs. No cross-job dependencies, no fan-in, no
        scheduler-level graphs. The fleet already has a durable workflow
        engine on a Postgres with run states, heartbeats and replay counts
        - glochid over PP run rows - and agent work belongs there.

        Auto-disable. Refused, see subsection policies, with the reason:
        a disabled job is an invisible outage carrying its own alibi.
       |))))

 (section acceptance
  (doc "Falsifiable gates; each one kills a specific way this design could be a lie")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        A1 harbor-kill: stop tuna, run a job. Row lands locally, ships
        after tuna returns, exactly one row on the far side. Proves the
        local-first law.

        A2 netless: no route to central, jobs still fire on schedule for an
        hour, outbox grows, drains on reconnect with no duplicates.

        A3 daemon-kill: SIGKILL the daemon. Central reports a heartbeat gap
        within 3x the interval AND per-job missing rows are NOT synthesized
        for the dead window (the box was dead, the jobs were not). Proven
        by asserting incident count and missing-row count separately.

        A4 duplicate-post: post the same batch twice; one row, updated
        received_at, no error. Post a superseding payload for the same
        (box, run_id); the later state wins.

        A5 bad-config: corrupt one schedule file; `check` exits nonzero;
        SIGHUP keeps the previous job set running and logs the refusal.
        Proves fail-closed reload.

        A6 timeout-orphan: job spawns a grandchild that outlives the
        deadline; after the kill, the process group is empty (ps check) and
        the verdict is timeout.

        A7 chatty: job writes 1 GiB; no deadlock, log_bytes reports the
        true size, log_tail is the last 8 KiB.

        A8 clock-step: step the wall clock forward 2h and back 2h under a
        running daemon; no firing storm, no skipped occurrence, occurrence
        values match the schedule.

        A9 retention: prune with unsent outbox rows present; the runs
        table shrinks and the outbox is untouched.

        A10 confinement: gregor's token posting box=wyo is refused whole
        (400), and no partial rows land.

        A11 false-missing: a daily job with heartbeat 26h and one late
        fire does not produce a missing verdict; the synthetic row appears
        only after the declared gap and only for a live box window.

        Each gate names the failure it forbids. A chapter that can only be
        demonstrated by a working dashboard is not this chapter.
       |))))

 (section open-questions
  (doc "Recorded so the next session does not relitigate from scratch")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        . WireGuard topology: which of gregor, pricklypear.club,
          pricklypear.rocks, wyo.town can POST to tuna over TLS and which
          need a route. Blocking for install, not for design; the answer
          picks whether ingest is public-TLS or tunnel-only.
        . Root jobs: how many exist, and whether the doas named-command
          list stays small enough to review. If it cannot stay small, the
          alternative is a separate root-configured instance, which is a
          different security story.
        . Running-state rows: v1 ships terminal rows only. If overlap and
          in-flight visibility at central turn out to matter more than
          churn, ship the running row too and let the upsert handle it.
        . Where this book lives long-term: standalone pp-cron repo, or
          section ingest lifted into tuna's borg/ beside trace.borg. Both
          were on the table on 2026-11-10 and neither was decided; the
          split is deliberately written along that seam.
        . PP projection (subsection lens): which space, which prim type,
          and whether the board wants one row per run or per day.
       |))))
)
